asserted only via EffectiveLimit; (d) ListOrganizationUsage has TWO independent tenant-predicate legs (one in the policies CTE on organization_id, one in the usage CTE on organization_id) — they need INDEPENDENT proofs because either leg dropping alone leaks a different column (limit_value vs used_value), so the file has BOTH a "policies-CTE leg isolated" test (orgA has an override, orgB has overrides on different resources, no counters) AND a "usage-CTE leg isolated" test (both tenants have a quota_usage counter on the SAME resource with DISTINCT used_value). NO per-id Get => NO (Code, Hint) existence-leak projection: the cross-tenant probe surfaces as an EMPTY projection (resolver returns ok=false, or len(rows)==0), NOT a typed apierr.NotFound — the empty-slice / ok=false IS the existence-indistinguishability proof. The shared-conflict-tuple fixture for the Upsert-UPDATE bystander test MUST be built via upsertOrganizationPolicy (not seedOrgPolicy raw SQL), because using the repository to build the fixture both proves the partial unique index lets the peer row land at all AND aligns baseline.updated_at timestamps so a wrong-row UPDATE shows up as sub-second drift. The corollary "same-resource-in-two-tenants-both-succeed" test is the load-bearing positive-direction pair — without it, a regression that resolved the conflict by (resource,) alone would have prevented the bystander INSERT, and every byte-identical assertion in the UPDATE-branch test would silently pass against a non-existent row. Helper naming caveat: nullable-column helpers MUST get a per-file suffix (stringPtrEqualQP / fmtStringPtrQP) — BE-0440's project-grant file already defines a stringPtrEqual variant in store_test and same-named helpers collide at compile time. Forward-applicable to any future dual-scope ledger-style table (a hypothetical KindFeatureFlagDefault + KindFeatureFlagOverride pairing, or KindPriceTier defaults + organization overrides, would inherit this template).
