canonical `kinds` map at the bottom — Valid() and the ID parser read from the map, not the const list, so a missing map entry silently breaks NewID(KindFoo) with ErrUnknownKind. For brand-new tables, services already exposes UNIQUE (organization_id, id) from migration 0002 so NO companion ALTER TABLE is needed on the parent (UNLIKE BE-0435 api_keys parent). Forward-applicable to any future grant table whose parent IS the leaf of the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy or whose parent is otherwise structurally leaf-like (a hypothetical KindDeploymentGrant / KindJobGrant would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:50:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped child table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert PLUS a bulk DELETE-by-exclusion (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete, no traditional UPDATE), with a database-managed version column, optional nullable scoping pointers, and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0439 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go). Two structural divergences from BE-0435 (api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go) are load-bearing: (1) the Upsert ON CONFLICT branch must be tested with a DIFFERENT caller-supplied id AND a DIFFERENT caller-supplied principal_kind to prove BOTH the caller-id-is-ignored guarantee AND the cross-kind-smuggling-impossible guarantee — the principal_kind column is NOT part of the scope tuple, so an existing row stays at its current principal_kind even when the caller passes a different kind for the same principal_id; a single Role-only update test would silently pass while a regression that promoted principal_kind to a SET clause still went undetected. (2) The Upsert rollback proof reduces to ListByProject-only because the repository exposes no per-id Get for grants — ListByProject is the ONLY observation surface for a grant row, so it is the load-bearing leak channel a partial commit would surface through, and an absent Get assertion is a deliberate file boundary, not a gap. Insert duplicate-id test must use a DIFFERENT principal_id so the ON CONFLICT (org, proj, principal, env, svc) branch does NOT match and the PRIMARY KEY (id) uniqueness violation is exercised — otherwise the upsert path silently swallows the duplicate id. DeleteByProjectExceptIDs has TWO codepaths in the repository (len(keepIDs)==0 -> unconditional DELETE; len>0 -> DELETE WHERE id NOT IN (ANY $3::text[])), so BOTH must be exercised independently — the empty-keep test must additionally use a fresh seed after the nil-keep clear AND assert the empty-slice ([]string{}) form is identical to nil at the wire (the repository documents nil and empty as identical, so a regression that diverged them would surface here). ON DELETE CASCADE from projects is the project-grant analogue of api_key_scope's ON DELETE CASCADE from api_keys: use raw `DELETE FROM projects WHERE id=$1` (NOT the repository's ScheduleDeletion, which is a SOFT delete and would NOT trigger CASCADE) and observe via ListByProject. Helper `newProjectGrantID(f, label)` synthesises a `pgrnt_<token_n>` id by rewriting f.Project's `prj_<token_n>` prefix — the testutil.Factory only stamps known domain prefixes and there is no f.ProjectGrant, so prefix-rewriting is the cleanest way to mint factory-unique ids without reaching into domain.NewID(domain.KindProjectGrant). The sibling project_grant_test.go's `seedProjectGrant` raw-SQL helper is kept intentionally separate: that file's tests exercise the read path against rows the schema (not the repository) inserted, so a regression that broke INSERT would still let the read tests pass — by contrast THIS file's `upsertProjectGrant` exercises the repository's INSERT path, so the two seed surfaces are complementary, not duplicates.
ralph/progress.txt:53:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped child table with a database-managed version column, a composite FK to a parent api_keys row, and NO secret-bearing column** (BE-0436 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_scope_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0430 membership_tenant_isolation_test.go shape is the closest template; four substitutions are mandatory: (a) the cross-tenant indistinguishability probe uses the SAME (Code, Hint) anchor pattern, but on a single id column rather than a composite key — both the cross-tenant scope id and an unknown scope id under the prober's tenant must render byte-identical typed NotFound; (b) the byte-identical-bystander assertion carries TWO independent trigger anchors (api_key_scopes_set_updated_at AND api_key_scopes_bump_version) instead of memberships' one (memberships_set_updated_at + role_version-only-via-UpdateRole). Both must be asserted because a regression could drift one without the other; (c) the load-bearing worst-case fixture is the shared-scope-text fixture (both tenants own a scope row with the SAME scope text on their OWN respective keys) because the (organization_id, api_key_id, scope) UNIQUE constraint is per-tenant, so the WHERE filter is the only thing keeping the rows apart at the read path — every byte-identical-bystander test uses this fixture as a result; (d) ListByAPIKey is a SECOND tenant-scoped list path (the parent api_key_id is the secondary tenant-scoping anchor) and must be tested both for cross-tenant api_key_id (returns empty non-nil slice) AND indistinguishable from an unknown api_key_id under the prober's tenant. The api_key_scopes table has NO secret-bearing column (the scope text itself is the customer-facing capability string), so the BE-0434 raw-secret_hash probe has no analogue and the byte-identical-bystander assertion alone covers every observable column. Helpers seedAPIKeyForScopes / newAPIKeyScope / insertAPIKeyScope are reused from api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go in the same store_test package — sibling-file test name collisions (e.g. TestAPIKeyScopeRepositoryListByAPIKeyIsTenantScoped from BE-0435) MUST be checked with `grep '^func Test'` on the sibling invariants file before adding new test names. Future tenant-scoped child tables with a version column and no secret-bearing column (variables.value when is_secret=false, future per-scope capability tables, scoped grant link tables not yet sharded out) should reuse THIS file as the template; if the row body carries a secret-bearing column add the BE-0434 raw-column probe on top.
ralph/progress.txt:54:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped table with FOUR distinct mutation paths (Update / UpdateDisplayName / ScheduleDeletion / Restore), a nullable soft-delete column (deletion_scheduled_at), and a count surface the quota layer reads (CountByOrganization)** (BE-0438 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_tenant_isolation_test.go): this is the BE-0436 api_key_scope_tenant_isolation template extended with three projections api_key_scopes had no analogue for — (a) UpdateDisplayName needs its own OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB / CrossTenantBystanderIsByteIdentical pair because no other repo file proves the cross-tenant invariant on this surface; (b) the byte-identical-bystander helper carries DeletionScheduledAt as a fourth observable column on top of (version, updated_at, slug, display_name), so a cross-tenant ScheduleDeletion that stamped a peer nil→non-nil OR a cross-tenant Restore that cleared a peer non-nil→nil is caught even when triggers happened to look untouched (same-second match); (c) one composite SoftDeletedRowIsTenantScopedOnReadPaths test proves cross-tenant invisibility across ALL THREE read surfaces (Get / ListByOrganization / CountByOrganization) AND owner-tenant visibility across the same three in a single fixture — the projections collapse into one test because the SAME WHERE organization_id filter is what isolates the row on every read surface. Force BOTH fixtures to share slug text via direct `.Slug = "shared-slug"` overrides AFTER projectFixture(f.Project(...)), because the per-factory token in f.Project derives distinct slug text per tenant and a WHERE-on-slug-only regression would otherwise stay invisible. Two shared helpers at file scope (`getProjectOrFail`, `assertProjectByteIdentical`) mirror BE-0436's helper names — same shape, DeletionScheduledAt column added. The file's docstring MUST call out which legs are already proved elsewhere (cross-tenant NotFound code for Update / ScheduleDeletion / Restore is in project_test.go / project_delete_test.go / project_restore_test.go) and which are NEW HERE (byte-identical bystander on version + updated_at + DeletionScheduledAt, Code+Hint indistinguishability of cross-tenant vs unknown probes, per-tenant UNIQUE-shared-column coexistence, soft-delete tenant-scoping across all three read surfaces, Count tenant-scoping on the quota path) so a future reader does not look for missing tests where the proof is intentionally elsewhere.
ralph/progress.txt:55:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped grandchild table whose ONLY read surface is a parent-scoped ListByProject (no per-id Get, no per-id Delete), with TWO mutation paths (Upsert with ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE PLUS DeleteByProjectExceptIDs whose len(keepIDs)==0 and len>0 codepaths must each be exercised), a database-managed version column, optional nullable scoping pointers (environment_id, service_id), and a composite FK to a parent (organization_id, project_id) -> projects** (BE-0440 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_grant_tenant_isolation_test.go). This is the BE-0436 api_key_scope_tenant_isolation template adapted for a grandchild table whose conflict target itself includes (organization_id, project_id) AND a multi-column scope tuple. Five projections api_key_scopes had no analogue for are load-bearing here: (a) the byte-identical-bystander proof is observed through ListByProject (the only read surface) — there is no per-id Get like api_key_scopes had, so a helper getGrantFromListOrFail must scan the list response for the bystander id and fatal on absence; the implementation favours correctness over efficiency because the bystander lists are 1-2 rows by construction. (b) the byte-identical-bystander assertGrantByteIdentical helper must compare TWO nullable *string pointer columns (environment_id, service_id) in addition to the scalar columns — pair-helper stringPtrEqual / fmtStringPtr emit "<nil>" vs quoted string for failing assertions so nullability and value drift surface side-by-side; api_key_scopes had no pointer columns, so this is the first tenant-isolation file with a *string comparison. (c) the load-bearing worst-case fixture is the shared-scope-tuple fixture (both tenants own a grant row with the SAME (principal_id, environment_id, service_id) tuple on their OWN projects) AND the Upsert UPDATE-branch bystander test re-upserts with a DIFFERENT role + a fresh caller-supplied id to BOTH fire the ON CONFLICT branch on orgA AND prove the caller-id is ignored — a regression that resolved the conflict by scope-tuple alone (without organization_id and project_id in the conflict target) would have UPDATEd orgB on the peer-trigger fire. (d) DeleteByProjectExceptIDs needs TWO bystander tests because the repository has two SQL codepaths (one unconditional DELETE for nil/empty keepIDs, one DELETE WHERE id NOT IN ANY for non-empty keepIDs); the len>0 test must seed orgB grants whose id is NOT in keepIDs so a regression that dropped the tenant predicate would have deleted them through the "NOT IN ANY" branch. (e) ListByProject cross-tenant projectID returns an empty non-nil slice, so the cross-tenant indistinguishability projection is len==0 + non-nil + unknown-projectID-under-own-tenant yields the SAME empty-non-nil shape (NOT the Code+Hint NotFound shape from api_key_scopes / projects, because grants are read through a list path, not a per-id Get). Helpers seedOrg / seedProject / newProjectGrantID / upsertProjectGrant / listGrantsOrFail are reused from project_grant_test.go and project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go in the same store_test package — sibling-file test name collisions (TestProjectGrantRepo* in project_grant_test.go, TestProjectGrantRepository* in project_grant_repository_invariants_test.go) MUST be checked with `grep '^func Test'` on BOTH sibling files before adding new test names; this file uses the TestProjectGrantRepository* prefix (matching the invariants file convention) with unique suffixes (...OnOrgADoesNotTouchOrgB, ...IsolatesSharedPrincipal, ...InTwoTenantsBothSucceed). The file's docstring MUST call out which legs are already proved elsewhere (cross-tenant FK Conflict, duplicate-PK Conflict, ON DELETE CASCADE, tx-rollback, nil-Tx guards in BE-0439; ListByProject ordering and basic cross-tenant empty-list property in project_grant_test.go) and which are NEW HERE (byte-identical bystander on version + updated_at + role + pointer columns across BOTH Upsert branches AND BOTH DeleteByProjectExceptIDs codepaths, indistinguishability of cross-tenant vs unknown projectID at the list path, per-tenant scope-tuple coexistence, shared-principal_id read isolation) so a future reader does not look for missing tests where the proof is intentionally elsewhere.
ralph/progress.txt:58:- New customer-data resource = full vertical when it is the FIRST story for that table (BE-0435 pattern, api_key_scopes migration 0033, store/api_key_scope.go, api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go): this story is allowed to land migration + repository + first-pass tests because no prior story touched the table (no migration, no repo, no port, no handler, no wiring). The migration declares the table tenant-scoped at the database via the composite FK pattern established by project_grants / environment_grants — and when the parent table never exposed UNIQUE (organization_id, id) (api_keys was such a table) the SAME migration adds the constraint to the parent so the child's composite FK has a target; this is a safe additive change because the parent's PK already makes id unique and the new UNIQUE merely pairs it with organization_id. Repository follows the membership.go / project_grant.go pattern: stateless struct, *Tx for mutations with nil-Tx -> typed apierr.Internal, Querier for reads, pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound, mapWriteError for constraint-violation -> apierr.Conflict, tag.RowsAffected() == 0 -> apierr.NotFound on Delete. Tests combine CRUD + row-shape invariants in one file since no prior tests exist; tenant-isolation byte-identical-bystander proof is deferred to the paired BE-0436 (tenant-isolation) story that always follows.
ralph/progress.txt:59:- Test fixture caveat for api_keys-related seeding (BE-0435 learning, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_scope_repository_invariants_test.go seedAPIKeyForScopes): testutil.Factory.APIKey stamps a per-FACTORY prefix `yk_<token[:8]>`, NOT a per-key prefix. Two keys minted from the same factory get the same Prefix value, which collides with the api_keys_prefix_key UNIQUE constraint when two are inserted in the same test. Always derive the persisted prefix as `key.Prefix + "_" + key.ID` (or similar per-row salt) — never use `key.Prefix + "_" + label` because the same label is reused across orgs in tenant-isolation fixtures and the label is not unique.
ralph/progress.txt:60:- Repository for a **global identity table** with no organization_id and no version column (BE-0427 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/user.go): follow OrganizationRepository's shape (Get-by-own-id, scanX helper, nil-Tx guard → apierr.Internal) but drop the optimistic-concurrency If-Match path entirely; add a separate GetInOrganization(organizationID, userID) that JOINs against memberships so tenant-scoped reads return NotFound (the same shape an unknown id produces) for non-members and cross-tenant requesters. Delete uses RowsAffected() == 0 → apierr.NotFound (mirrors api_keys.Revoke). Conflict mapping for citext UNIQUE columns goes through mapWriteError with a non-sensitive user-facing message.
ralph/progress.txt:63:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **composite-key tenant-scoped link table** with a database-managed version column (BE-0429 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/membership_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go shape is the closest template, but four substitutions are mandatory because the row has no own-id, has a (organization_id, user_id) composite primary key, and carries role_version + the memberships_set_updated_at trigger: (a) the duplicate-Insert Conflict test asserts on the PK collision (a second Insert with the same (org, user) pair) instead of the citext-UNIQUE-email collision — the schema has no UNIQUE column outside the PK that an INSERT could violate; (b) the existing membership_test.go's UpdateRole case already asserts the role_version atomic +1 increment, so the invariants file's UpdateRole test is dedicated to the timestamp dual-anchor (updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE-UPDATE trigger, created_at preserved) — do NOT duplicate the role_version increment here; (c) NO Delete-cascades-to-children test (the membership row is the SINK of two ON-DELETE-CASCADE FKs from users and organizations, not a SOURCE — cascades INTO memberships are proved in the parent table's invariants file, e.g. TestUserRepositoryDeleteCascadesToMemberships in user_repository_invariants_test.go); (d) the local rollback sentinel type MUST use a distinct name from user_repository_invariants_test.go's `errSentinel` because every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same store_test package — `membershipTxRollbackSentinel struct{}` is the canonical naming choice. The Insert-returns-row-with-timestamps test additionally asserts (a) role_version == 1 (the schema default that backs session-token revocation in BE-0020 auth), (b) Email + UserDisplayName populated from the JOINed users row (Insert returns OrganizationMember, not raw Membership — the httpapi layer renders the joined shape directly), and (c) created_at.Equal(updated_at) on the fresh row (the trigger has not fired yet). Seed pattern: seedOrg + seedUserRow first, then call repo.Insert through the repository (NOT seedMembership, which is the raw-INSERT helper for tenant-isolation tests). Forward-applicable to BE-0431/0432 (service_accounts CRUD + tenant-isolation) and BE-0433/0434 (api_keys CRUD + tenant-isolation) — both are tenant-scoped tables with their own version-tracking columns, so the rule "split the version-increment assertion (canonical CRUD test) from the timestamp-dual-anchor assertion (invariants test)" should also apply.
ralph/progress.txt:64:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped table whose only customer mutation is an idempotent lifecycle flip** (BE-0431 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go and BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but three substitutions are mandatory because service_accounts has no generic Update and no version column — only Insert + Disable (COALESCE-idempotent): (a) the trigger-dual-anchor test drives through the **lifecycle mutation** (Disable) instead of a generic Update — created_at preserved, updated_at moves forward via the BEFORE UPDATE trigger, plus the lifecycle field (disabled_at) is set and IsDisabled() flips true; (b) the idempotent-second-call semantics are NOT re-asserted here (they belong in the canonical CRUD test in serviceaccount_test.go); (c) the rollback-leaves-zero-footprint test asserts BOTH Get-is-NotFound AND CountByOrganization == 0 (the quota reader is the second observable surface a half-written row could surface through, and a future refactor that switched Insert to a SAVEPOINT or autonomous transaction would silently inflate it without ever surfacing on Get). Distinct sentinel type name (`serviceAccountTxRollbackSentinel`) to avoid the store_test package-level collision with `errSentinel` (user) and `membershipTxRollbackSentinel` (membership). Forward-applicable to BE-0433/0434 (api_keys CRUD + tenant-isolation): api_keys has an Update path (rotate / revoke / patch metadata), so the invariants file should re-introduce the full Update-trigger dual-anchor test from the user/membership template alongside the rollback + nil-Tx guards; the secret-bearing nature of api_keys means the rollback test must additionally assert that no secret_hash needle leaks into post-rollback observable state.
ralph/progress.txt:65:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **composite-key tenant-scoped link table** with NO own id, NO version column independent of role_version, and NO soft-delete column (BE-0430 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/membership_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0428 user_tenant_isolation_test.go shape is the closest template, but four substitutions are mandatory because the link table is keyed by (organization_id, user_id) and the load-bearing cross-row failure mode is "the SAME user is a legitimate member of two tenants, and a WHERE-on-user-only mutation scrubs/bumps the WRONG tenant's row": (a) every byte-identical-snapshot test seeds the SAME user_id into BOTH tenants with deliberately distinct (role, role_version) tuples — a WHERE-on-user-only mistake would return one row for both Get calls and the disjoint role_version values would catch it even when the role values coincide; (b) the UpdateRole bystander test snapshots the OTHER tenant's row (not the unrelated user's row) — the BE-0428 "different user, different tenant" framing is too weak for a link table, because the disjoint-id check that user identity gets for free here is the WHOLE THING the test is proving; (c) the duplicate-Insert Conflict bystander test snapshots BOTH the conflicting target tenant's row AND the SAME-user-different-tenant bystander row — the PK collision fires AFTER any row-level CHECKs and the BEFORE-INSERT triggers, so a regression that ever touched the wrong row would leak through updated_at on either side; (d) the cross-tenant-NotFound-vs-unknown-pair indistinguishability test must run for BOTH Get AND GetMember (the JOIN-bearing read) — divergence in either Code or Hint is an existence leak surface, and the JOIN-bearing path is a separate code path from the bare-row Get. The List isolation test takes TWO shapes that must both run: (i) disjoint-user-base count isolation (orgA has 1 member, orgB has 3, List(orgA) returns 1 — proves the WHERE-organization_id filter is present at all); (ii) shared-user-row isolation (Ada is a member of BOTH tenants, List(orgA) returns ONE row containing only orgA's view of Ada, List(orgB) returns ONE row containing only orgB's view of Ada — proves the JOIN does not produce a row-per-(membership × every memberships row for the same user_id) duplication). Document the deliberate ABSENCE of soft-delete tests (memberships has no deletion_scheduled_at) AND the cross-table cascade tests (cascades INTO memberships from users/organizations are proved by the parent-table tests TestUserRepositoryDeleteCascadesToMemberships and TestTenantHierarchyCascadeDelete, not here) in the file's package-doc comment. CRITICAL SEED-VALUE GOTCHA: the memberships_role_check CHECK constraint on memberships restricts role to {owner, admin, member} ONLY — the broader {owner, admin, developer, viewer, ci, support} set is the GRANTS-table role enum (project_grants/environment_grants), not memberships. Tests that seed "developer" or "viewer" into memberships will fail with `new row for relation "memberships" violates check constraint "memberships_role_check" (SQLSTATE 23514)`. Forward-applicable to BE-0432 (service_accounts tenant isolation: a tenant-scoped table with an organization_id COLUMN and an own id, so the BE-0426 organizations template is the closer fit — but the "same logical entity across two tenants" angle does not apply to service_accounts the way it does to memberships, because a service_account row has no shared-identity column the way memberships shares user_id with the global users table) and BE-0434 (api_keys tenant isolation: same shape as service_accounts).
ralph/progress.txt:66:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, NO generic Update, and NO soft-delete column** — only Insert + Disable lifecycle flip (BE-0432 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceaccount_tenant_isolation_test.go). The BE-0428 user_tenant_isolation_test.go and BE-0430 membership_tenant_isolation_test.go shapes are the templates, but four substitutions are mandatory because service_accounts has its OWN id (no shared-identity column the way memberships shares user_id with the global users table) and the load-bearing cross-row failure mode is "two tenants happen to share a slug (UNIQUE (organization_id, slug) permits this by design) and a WHERE-on-slug-only or WHERE-on-id-only mutation swaps the WRONG tenant's row": (a) every byte-identical-snapshot test seeds BOTH tenants with service accounts holding the SAME slug via a `seedServiceAccountWithSlug(t, db, f, org, label, slug)` helper that overrides the factory's slug — a WHERE-on-slug mistake would resolve to whichever row the planner found first and the per-tenant id assertion would catch it (the BE-0430 "same user, distinct role/role_version tuples" framing translates here to "different ids, SAME slug"; the inverse is the only cross-row leak surface a tenant-root-with-its-own-id table exposes); (b) the Disable bystander test is the analogue of BE-0430's UpdateRole bystander — Disable is the only mutation beyond Insert, so the BE-0428 generic Update test does not apply; the trigger dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed) PLUS the lifecycle field (disabled_at == nil) on the bystander row catch any WHERE-on-slug-only Disable that ever matched the wrong row; (c) the duplicate-Insert Conflict bystander test snapshots BOTH the conflicting target tenant's row AND the same-slug-different-tenant bystander row AND a post-state CountByOrganization == 1 for each (since the trigger is BEFORE-UPDATE only, a rolled-back INSERT that resolved its UNIQUE check against the cross-tenant row would have no trigger anchor — Count is the second observable surface a row landing in the wrong tenant could leak through, mirroring the BE-0431 invariants-file Count-after-rollback rule); (d) the cross-tenant-NotFound-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability test must run for BOTH Get AND Disable (the only two NotFound-emitting paths the repository exposes) — divergence in Code or Hint is an existence leak surface, and the mutation path is a separate code path from the read; afterward, re-read the foreign tenant's row and assert disabled_at == nil so the cross-tenant Disable probe did not silently flip the lifecycle. The List isolation test takes TWO shapes that must both run (mirrors BE-0430): (i) disjoint-fixture count isolation (orgA=1 SA, orgB=3 SAs, gotA must contain only saA's id, gotB must contain exactly {saB1, saB2, saB3} — proves no JOIN scrambled which rows land where); (ii) shared-slug-row isolation (saA and saB both with slug "shared-ci-slug", List(orgA) returns exactly one row with id=saA, List(orgB) returns exactly one row with id=saB — proves a SELECT disambiguating by slug instead of by organization would have either swapped or merged the two rows). One additional CountByOrganization-specific test seeds three tenants (orgA=2, orgB=3, orgC=0 empty bystander) and asserts the three distinct counts; this is the quota-layer leak surface (Count is the read the quota engine consults to enforce per-organization limits, so a leak inflates one tenant's effective quota with another's usage). Document the deliberate ABSENCE of soft-delete tests (service_accounts has no deletion_scheduled_at — only disabled_at, which the byte-identical Disable snapshot treats as the analogue) AND the deliberate ABSENCE of generic-Update / DELETE / cascade tests (Disable is the only mutation; the api_keys.service_account_id cross-tenant FK leak proof lives in serviceaccount_test.go's TestServiceAccountAPIKeyCannotCrossTenantBoundary; the cascade-on-org-delete proof lives in TestServiceAccountBelongsToOneOrganization and TestTenantHierarchyCascadeDelete) in the file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0428/BE-0430's deliberate-absence note). Note on apierr.NotFound message vs Hint: NotFound("service_account", id) Newf-builds a Message containing the caller-supplied id verbatim, but never sets Hint — so the cross-tenant-vs-unknown-id indistinguishability check MUST compare Code and Hint only (Message would diverge by caller input, which is not a leak). Forward-applicable to BE-0434 (api_keys tenant-isolation: same shape with the same "two tenants share a slug-equivalent" angle — api_keys has UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix), so the shared-prefix fixture is the structural counterpart; plus a secret-bearing extension — the cross-tenant probes must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface) and BE-0436 (api_key_scopes tenant-isolation).
ralph/progress.txt:67:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **tenant-scoped row with its own id, organization_id COLUMN, NO version column, THREE distinct UPDATE paths (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential), an additional UPDATE-but-not-mutation-state path (TouchLastUsed), and a secret-bearing row body (secret_hash + globally-UNIQUE prefix)** (BE-0433 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_repository_invariants_test.go). The BE-0427 user_repository_invariants_test.go, BE-0429 membership_repository_invariants_test.go, and BE-0431 serviceaccount_repository_invariants_test.go shapes are the templates, but five substitutions are mandatory because api_keys has no version column, three UPDATE paths instead of one, a separate touch-style UPDATE for the auth path, and a secret-bearing row body: (a) the trigger-dual-anchor (created_at preserved + updated_at refreshed by the BEFORE UPDATE trigger) must run ONCE PER UPDATE PATH — UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, and TouchLastUsed each get their own dedicated dual-anchor test, so a refactor that dropped the trigger from one path but not the others trips a path-specific gate (the BE-0431 single-Disable pattern is too weak here); (b) the UpdateMutable dual-anchor test additionally pins the **mutable surface** to exactly {name, scopes} by snapshotting every non-mutable column (prefix, secret_hash, created_by, service_account_id, expires_at, revoked_at, last_used_at) as byte-identical to the baseline — UpdateMutable is the first repository method in the store package whose SET-clause is a true subset of the table's mutable columns, so the non-mutation assertion is the only structural defense against a future refactor that quietly widened the SET-clause; (c) RotateCredential gets a dedicated test that pins the **credential swap** invariant: prefix and secret_hash flip to the new values, every identity/ownership/scopes/lifecycle column stays byte-identical, the OLD prefix no longer FindByPrefix-resolves (must surface yerr.CodeNotFound — rotation is the only mutation that orphans a previous lookup key), and the NEW prefix re-binds to the SAME key id (rotation is a credential swap, not a re-mint, so audit trails and outstanding access grants keep pointing at the same id); (d) the rollback-leaves-zero-footprint test asserts the standard Get-NotFound + ListByOrganization-empty, AND adds two **secret-bearing redaction probes** specific to api_keys: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys WHERE secret_hash = $1 must return 0 AND SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys WHERE prefix = $1 must return 0 — a SAVEPOINT-or-autonomous-transaction refactor that survived the outer rollback would surface here even when every per-id read reports NotFound, because the credential primitives (the secret hash an attacker would need, and the public prefix that drives the authentication lookup) are the load-bearing observables the table owes its caller; (e) typed-Internal nil-Tx guards run for ALL FIVE mutation paths (Insert / UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential / TouchLastUsed) — the pre-existing TestAPIKeyRepositoryInsertRejectsNilTx in apikey_test.go only asserts err != nil (weaker than the typed-Internal contract every other repository follows), so the typed-code test is additive, not a replacement. Distinct sentinel type name (`apiKeyTxRollbackSentinel`) to avoid the store_test package-level collision with `errSentinel` (user), `membershipTxRollbackSentinel` (membership), and `serviceAccountTxRollbackSentinel` (service account). Local `equalTimePtr(a, b *time.Time) bool` helper is the canonical way to express "lifecycle stamp untouched" concisely without dragging in a third-party deep-equality helper — both-nil OR both-non-nil-and-time.Equal. Forward-applicable to BE-0434 (api_keys tenant isolation: the cross-tenant probes must additionally assert no secret_hash needle reaches any observable surface — same secret-bearing redaction probe pattern documented above for the rollback test, but applied to the cross-tenant-Get / cross-tenant-FindByPrefix / cross-tenant-List read paths and to the typed error surfaces Code/Hint/Message) and to ANY future repository whose row body carries a secret-bearing column (api_key_scopes does NOT — the scope-name column is non-secret — but variables.value when is_secret=true IS, so the BE-0451+ variables invariants stories should reuse this secret-bearing-rollback-probe rule).
ralph/progress.txt:68:- Repository tenant-isolation test file for a **tenant-scoped row whose mutation surface spans FIVE distinct methods (UpdateMutable / Revoke / RotateCredential / TouchLastUsed plus the Insert path) AND whose row body carries TWO load-bearing credential primitives (a globally-UNIQUE prefix and a secret-bearing secret_hash)** (BE-0434 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/api_key_tenant_isolation_test.go; the cross-tenant analogue of BE-0433 and the secret-bearing variant of the BE-0432 service_accounts shape). Two schema-level facts make this pattern structurally distinct from BE-0432: (i) api_keys.prefix is GLOBALLY UNIQUE (NOT UNIQUE (organization_id, prefix) — the prefix authentication lookup runs BEFORE the caller's tenant is known) so the BE-0432 "two tenants share a slug" worst-case fixture has no row-level analogue here; the cross-tenant prefix-conflict path is still load-bearing (orgA Insert with orgB's already-persisted prefix AND orgA RotateCredential to orgB's prefix) and BOTH paths must surface CodeConflict, leave orgB's bystander row byte-identical, and leave NO row anywhere carrying the rolled-back secret_hash needle (raw-SQL probe). (ii) api_keys carries a secret-bearing column — secret_hash — so the cross-tenant byte-identical-bystander assertion includes a `assertRawSecretHashUnchanged` raw-SQL probe AGAINST the typed-read SecretHash field (the typed read path is already covered by the struct snapshot, but a regression that scanned a non-secret column into the struct or skipped the field on Get would silently pass the struct check while the underlying row drifted — the raw probe is the load-bearing safety net for THAT class of regression specifically), and the cross-tenant indistinguishability test additionally asserts the secret_hash needle does NOT appear in any of the typed-error surfaces yeCross.Error() / yeCross.Message / yeCross.Hint across ALL five mutation methods (the secret-bearing redaction probe extension to the BE-0432 Code+Hint indistinguishability rule). Skeleton: one t.Parallel test per (mutation × leg) plus a 5-row indistinguishability-and-redaction table test that iterates (Get, UpdateMutable, Revoke, RotateCredential, TouchLastUsed). Three load-bearing rules: (1) Every byte-identical-bystander test asserts BOTH the trigger-managed updated_at (the api_keys_set_updated_at BEFORE-UPDATE trigger refreshes it on every matched UPDATE, including a WHERE-on-id-only UPDATE that touched the wrong tenant) AND the lifecycle column the OWNING method writes (revoked_at for Revoke, last_used_at for TouchLastUsed, prefix+secret_hash for RotateCredential, name+scopes for UpdateMutable) so a WHERE-on-id-only regression surfaces independently on EITHER anchor. (2) The Insert cross-tenant prefix-conflict test must add a SELECT COUNT(*) FROM api_keys global-table-size probe AFTER the conflicting Insert — a rolled-back Insert that landed its row in some OTHER tenant's slot would show up there even if the per-id probe missed it; the per-id probe alone is insufficient because the failing row's id is not known to the probe. (3) FindByPrefix is INTENTIONALLY NOT a cross-tenant probe surface — the signature carries no caller-tenant parameter, the prefix column is globally unique, and the resolved row carries its own organization_id; document this absence in the test file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors BE-0426's "tenant-root tables have no ListByParent" note). Helper shape worth pinning forward: a `getXOrFail(ctx, t, s, repo, orgID, id, label) X` and a `writeX(ctx, s, fn) (X, error)` pair keep byte-identical snapshot tests free of Read/Write closure boilerplate; combined with a single `assertBystanderByteIdentical(t, label, baseline, after)` helper they reduce a 12-column equality check to two lines per (mutation × leg). Forward-applicable to BE-0436 (api_key_scopes tenant isolation — a child-table variant with NO secret-bearing column and only one mutation surface, so the secret-bearing redaction probe and most of the conflict-rollback shape collapse; the LIST-by-parent isolation rule remains) and to BE-0450 (variables tenant isolation, where the secret-bearing column is value WHEN is_secret=true, so the rolled-back-secret probe rule applies CONDITIONALLY on is_secret — the same shape extended with a per-row classification check). Use docker compose up -d postgres + YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestAPIKeyRepository.*OrgADoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryGetReturnsCorrectRowAcrossTenants|TestAPIKeyRepositoryInsertCrossTenantPrefixConflictDoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryRotateCredentialCrossTenantPrefixConflictDoesNotTouchOrgB|TestAPIKeyRepositoryListByOrganizationCountsAreIsolated|TestAPIKeyRepositoryListByServiceAccountIsTenantScoped|TestAPIKeyRepositoryCrossTenantErrorIsIndistinguishableFromUnknownId' ./internal/controlplane/store/ to verify; the suite skips cleanly when the env var is unset. Same pre-existing -race-with-DB unrelated regressions noted under BE-0426/BE-0432 still apply — confirmed by stashing the new file and re-running, the failing tests are environment-service / api-key-service Create paths, organization_variable_patch audit metadata, and TestSQLArgsAreConstantExpressions; none are touched by this pattern.
ralph/progress.txt:168:- Repository tenant-isolation for a tenant-root table (BE-0426 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/organization_tenant_isolation_test.go; structural template for BE-0428/0430/0432/0434/0436 and every future tenant-root-table isolation story): the strongest isolation guarantee in the codebase lives on the row-id-keyed tenant root (organizations) and the proof shape is "byte-identical snapshot of the OTHER tenant across every mutation path the repository exposes". Skeleton: one Go test file per table, one t.Parallel test per (mutation × leg) — Get returns-only-named-tenant + Get unknown-id-is-NotFound (cross-tenant + missing render the same yerr.CodeNotFound — no oracle); Update only-touches-named-tenant (snapshot orgB, Update orgA, re-read orgB, assert byte-identical across slug, display_name, version, updated_at, deletion_scheduled_at; assert orgA's update DID land so a no-op repository can't pass trivially); Update-with-mismatched-If-Match also-does-not-touch-orgB (catches "fall back to unconditional UPDATE on stale" regressions); ScheduleDeletion only-touches-named-tenant (stamp on orgA, orgB byte-identical incl. DeletionScheduledAt == nil); Get-of-soft-deleted-tenant-does-not-mask-other (soft-delete is per-row, not SQL-level hide; the scheduled row is still readable and orgB unchanged); Insert enforces-global-slug-uniqueness (typed yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty user-facing Message; first row's slug unchanged; second row's id is yerr.CodeNotFound on follow-up Get — the failed Insert lands no row). Three load-bearing rules: (1) anchor on BOTH version (trigger-bumped on every UPDATE so a missing WHERE bumps every row) AND updated_at (trigger-refreshed; same property) — either one catches a WHERE-less UPDATE independently of the other, keep both; (2) the "stale-If-Match" leg is structurally distinct from the "successful update" leg and exercises the conflict-detection path which historically falls back to extra reads/writes that could leak across tenants; (3) Insert conflict-message assertions anchor on yerr.CodeConflict + non-empty Message ONLY, never on a substring (the static "an organization with this slug already exists" message is a stable contract but story-specific prose checks are brittle). Tenant-root tables have NO ListByParent method at this layer (no parent to scope by) — the "list queries don't leak total counts" acceptance criterion is vacuously satisfied for tenant-root tables; the corresponding proof for /v1/me/organizations lives in membership tests. Document this absence in the test file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test. CHILD-TABLE variant (BE-0428+ for users/memberships/projects/environments/services/api_keys/etc.) extends this skeleton with one additional leg: a cross-tenant parent_id probe — passing org A's parent_id (project_id, etc.) alongside org B's organization_id must render NotFound, NEVER A's row, NEVER a 500 leaking the cause. Use docker compose up -d postgres + YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://yalla:yalla@127.0.0.1:5432/yalla go test -race -count=1 -run 'TestXxxRepository' to verify; the suite skips cleanly when the env var is unset, so CI without Postgres remains green. Known pre-existing -race-with-DB failures in environment-service / api-key-service Create paths and organization_variable_patch audit metadata are NOT introduced by this pattern (confirmed by stashing the new test file and re-running each failing case); a focused triage story should pick them up before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
ralph/progress.txt:183:- PUT-replace-variables contract suite on a bare-{id}-resolver route (BE-0175 contract pattern, internal/controlplane/httpapi/environment_variables_put_test.go): canonical 12-test contract suite for ANY customer-facing PUT-replace-{secret-bearing-children} endpoint sitting on a bare-{id}-resolver route. Structural HYBRID of (a) environment_grants_put_test.go (BE-0169 contract pattern — route-shape twin: same env-id resolver, same NewHandler positional port placement, same missing-field-400 + malformed-400 + unknown-field-400 + unauth-401 + viewer-403 + store NotFound 404 + store InvalidInput 400 + store unavailable 503 + unwired 500 + OpenAPI registration skeleton) and (b) project_variables_test.go (BE-0142 GET pattern — resource-shape twin: output.Sentinel projection of is_secret rows, secret-plaintext leak guards on response bodies including the seeded substring). The pattern adds TWO load-bearing assertions BE-0169 (a grants-only resource) cannot supply: (1) the happy-path body POSITIVELY asserts got0.Value == output.Sentinel for the secret row AND negatively asserts the body does not contain the seeded plaintext — the wire chokepoint must fire on the post-write re-read even though the caller just submitted the plaintext; both directions catch different regressions. (2) A dedicated TestDeniesSupportInTenant — CapWrite has no support exception (the CapRead cross-tenant exception is one-way), so a CapWrite contract suite MUST positively assert support in their own tenant is denied 403, locking the deny against a future engine regression that widens CapWrite to support. apierr.InvalidInput wire shape: the renderer emits FieldViolation as "<field>: <reason>" inside the hint, NOT as a JSON key with quotes — a contract test that asserts the field path reaches the wire must use the substring "variables: " (with space), NOT "variables" wrapped in double-quotes; the success envelope DOES use "variables" as a JSON key but the error envelope serialises the field path into a human-readable hint instead. The handlerFor uses the trailing replacer port (everything else is fakeXxx{} zero-value); the all-nils logger is nil to silence test output. The future PUT-replace-{secrets} story on api_keys, service_variables, or any other credential-bearing child resource is a verbatim clone of this 12-test skeleton with the resource type swapped and the action const swapped.
ralph/progress.txt:229:- Customer-facing soft-delete endpoints come in two shapes. Organization.delete uses a deletion_scheduled_at stamp + 202 Accepted (asynchronous teardown by a future worker; re-scheduling is apierr.Conflict). API-key DELETE is a one-way revocation: the api_keys.revoked_at column is stamped and the credential is permanently unusable from that moment (the auth-layer IsUsable check already rejects a revoked row), the row stays so the audit trail of who minted it stays linked to it, response is 200 OK with the revoked snapshot (mirroring members.Remove's terminal-view convention). The repository (APIKeyRepository.Revoke) is silently idempotent at SQL via COALESCE(revoked_at, $now), but the service layer (APIKeyService.Revoke) pre-checks current.IsRevoked() inside the tx and returns apierr.Conflict on re-revoke — the customer's view of the lifecycle is stale and a silent success would write a misleading audit record. Pattern: customer endpoints surface stale-lifecycle conflicts; repository methods stay SQL-idempotent for non-customer callers (workers, admin jobs).
ralph/progress.txt:244:- Hard-delete vs soft-delete decision rule: a customer-facing resource takes a SOFT delete (deletion_scheduled_at on organizations + 202 Accepted, revoked_at on api_keys + 200 OK with revocation snapshot) ONLY when the row carries a continuing tie that must outlive the resource — an audit-log FK that must keep the row reachable, a child cascade that must be ordered carefully, or a "lifecycle stale" property the customer's audit trail depends on. A resource with no continuing tie (the audit log records the deletion independently, no child tables, no FK ties) takes a HARD delete with a 200 OK response carrying the row's snapshot exactly as it stood at the moment of removal (DELETE /v1/organizations/{org_id}/variables/{key}, BE-0115). The store repository method uses `DELETE FROM ... WHERE org=$1 AND key=$2 RETURNING ...` so one round trip removes the row AND yields the snapshot the orchestrator returns to the wire; pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound (a cross-tenant key is indistinguishable from a missing row). The service files an audit event in the SAME tx as the delete, with Metadata={variable_id: row.ID} — customer-supplied key + the deleted value are never recorded, so the audit row is auditable without ever observing the variable name or its value. An agent can confirm what was deleted (id, key, version, timestamps) without a separate read; 204 No Content would have been less useful here.
ralph/progress.txt:255:- Inbound HTTP rate-limit pattern (BE-0035, internal/controlplane/ratelimit/limiter.go + internal/controlplane/httpapi/ratelimit.go + apierr.RateLimited + config.RateLimit): the limiter is an in-process multi-dimensional token-bucket gate (per-org, per-API-key, per-IP) with read/write-class specs derived from the HTTP method (GET/HEAD/OPTIONS = read, everything else = write). Three load-bearing rules: (1) the gate wraps every route INSIDE RequireAuth in server.go's per-route loop (`h = rateLimit(h); if RequiresAuth: h = RequireAuth(...)(h)`) so the resolved principal (org id, key id, auth method) is on the request context when the limiter decides — public endpoints fall back to the IP bucket alone; (2) the internal-worker exemption is wired through a new package-private context helper `withAuthMethod` / `AuthMethodFromContext` in middleware.go that BOTH RequireAuth and RequireInternalWorker stamp with id.Method, so the rate-limit gate can recognise auth.MethodInternalWorker and pass `Exempt=true` to the limiter without re-running the credential store; (3) the "first exhausted bucket wins" rule short-circuits downstream debits (org denied -> key & ip buckets untouched) so a single throttled tenant cannot drain a shared NAT'd IP bucket of every neighbour. Bucket map is sync.Mutex'd with lazy eviction on the request path (no background goroutine; pruning sweeps every IdleTTL); the freshly-created bucket MUST set lastUsed=now BEFORE the sweep runs so the sweep cannot reap the bucket it just inserted (initial zero-value lastUsed is older than any cutoff and was a real bug caught by tests). The 429 wire shape is the standard yalla.error.v1 envelope with apierr.RateLimited(scope, retry) attaching `scope` (bucket dimension name only — "organization" / "api_key" / "ip", NEVER the tenant id or key id or IP) and `retry_after` (integer seconds, clamped to >=1 because the wire Retry-After header cannot carry sub-second); the response also sets the matching `Retry-After: N` header. NewHandler grew ONE new trailing parameter (RateLimiter) — sweep all 64 NewHandler call sites in tests via a Node script that walks the source with depth-tracking parens and appends `, nil` before each closing `)`. Config plumbing: new YALLA_RATE_LIMIT_* env vars (ORG/KEY/IP × READ/WRITE × RPS/BURST + IDLE_TTL + DISABLED master switch) layered over per-profile defaults; ProfileTest defaults to Disabled=true so contract tests across the whole repo never accidentally trip the limiter. The limiter constructor takes a `func() time.Time` clock injection point so deterministic tests (`fixedClock.Advance(d)`) can drive refill behaviour without sleeping. Forward-applicable to any future throughput cap (per-resource, per-tenant-tier, per-route-specific) — extend Specs/Specs.For pairs rather than adding a new bucket dimension. Redaction backstops: the 429 envelope, the Retry-After header, the WARN log record on denial, and every rate-limit identifier flow through telemetry.LogAttrs / apienvelope's regex-based scrubber so a bearer-token in the Authorization header on a throttled request NEVER reaches a log file (TestRateLimitDenialLogsNoSecretValue is the regression pin).
ralph/progress.txt:274:- Quota-dimension story whose call site is NOT yet wired (BE-0337 api_keys pattern, internal/controlplane/store/apikeyservice.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0027_quota_resource_api_keys.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/apikey_quota_api_keys_concurrent_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go; structural template for BE-0338 members and ANY future dimension story whose owning Service.Create predates the QuotaReserver port — applies whenever a dimension story is the FIRST to wire its service-layer Reserve, distinct from the BE-0331..BE-0336 'call site already there' siblings whose owning unit-of-work already reserves the dimension). The minimum atomic delivery is FOUR change axes: (1) closed-set expansion — a new `NNNN_quota_resource_<name>.{up,down}.sql` migration that follows the BE-0326 backup_schedules template verbatim (DO block looking up the inline DOMAIN CHECK by `contypid = 'quota_resource'::regtype AND contype = 'c'` so the migration is robust to any Postgres-assigned constraint name, then `ALTER DOMAIN quota_resource ADD CONSTRAINT quota_resource_check CHECK (VALUE IN (...))` with the prior set + the new label; the .down.sql is structurally identical and narrows back to the prior set, accepting that any row carrying the new label fails the restored CHECK — that is the correct behaviour, silently dropping rows would lose audit-relevant state); plus the matching `QuotaResource<Name>` constant + membership-set entry in internal/controlplane/store/quota.go; plus updating the `valid` slice in internal/controlplane/store/quota_internal_test.go::TestQuotaResourceValid which pins `len(valid) == len(quotaResources)` — the test is a sealing-trap that catches a forgotten membership-set entry but ALSO fires on a forgotten valid-slice entry; both must change together. (2) Constructor port widening — the existing service struct grows a `quota QuotaReserver` field, the constructor parameter list grows the same port, the nil-guard switch grows a `case quota == nil` arm placed BETWEEN the last existing repo arm and the audit arm (the convention is store → parent repos → own repo → authz? → quota → jobs? → audit), and the Create unit of work grows a `svc.quota.Reserve(ctx, tx, key.OrganizationID, string(QuotaResource<Name>))` call placed AFTER every existence/parent-lookup check and BEFORE the desired-state Insert so a 404 on the parent is never disguised as a quota rejection AND a row-level conflict (slug, prefix, unique constraint) rolls the reservation back with the rest of the unit of work. (3) Constructor call-site fanout — three call sites change: cmd/yalla-api/main.go grows a `<name>Quota := noopQuotaReserver{}` placeholder (the real quota.Checker adapter is wired separately by a cross-cutting plan-resolver story; placeholder mirrors the projectQuota / environmentQuota / serviceQuota convention exactly), the in-package `package store` constructor-nil test grows a `nopQuotaReserver{}` local + a new `{nil quota, ...}` case wedged BETWEEN the last existing nil case and the audit case (positional ordering must mirror the constructor's nil-guard switch), and the `package store_test` integration-test helper grows the shared `&recordingQuota{}` (defined in projectservice_test.go — not a local `noopQuotaReserver{}`, which is only visible to cmd/yalla-api; the store_test package has its OWN allow-all fake hierarchy and the convention is to reuse `recordingQuota` for happy-path constructors). (4) Concurrent integration test — clone of the BE-0336 concurrent_deployments test with five structural simplifications when the owning service has no Authorizer / JobEnqueuer ports (api_keys is pure Yalla identity with no Dokploy mirror, so APIKeyService takes neither): no `atomicAuthorizer` / `atomicJobs` fakes need to be defined or counted; no `authz.calls == attempts` and no `jobs.calls == successes` assertions; the per-goroutine input loop calls `auth.Generate()` INSIDE the goroutine so every concurrent attempt mints its own unique (prefix, secret_hash) pair via crypto/rand (the prefix column is UNIQUE so a shared pair would deterministically conflict and mask the quota race); the parent-row seed is only an `organizations` row (no project/environment/service chain — api_keys live directly under the tenant); and the leaky-input candidates in the rejection-message scan use the resource-specific prefix (`key-%02d` / `req_apikeys_%02d` / `corr_apikeys_%02d`) so an echoed input still fails the message guard. The 'second test' sibling (TestXxxCreateAppliesXxxQuotaIndependentlyOfOtherDimensions) seeds ONLY the dimension under enforcement, exhausts it sequentially, asserts a SIXTH Create surfaces the dimension-specific rejection, and asserts no reservation row exists on TWO unrelated dimensions (services / projects) — the symmetry guard that catches a future regression that drops the Reserve call OR accidentally chose a different resource string. The whole pattern compiles + passes -race against a real Postgres without touching ANY of the 70+ httpapi fake-fixture call sites because the service's HTTP layer is owned by a separate seam (the api_keys handler already calls APIKeyService.Create with the same input shape; only the store-layer wiring changes). Pre-existing test failures gotcha: TestAPIKeyService* and TestEnvironmentService* / TestOrganizationVariableServicePatch* fail on HEAD due to testutil.Factory id-prefix vs ParseSlug drift — confirmed by stashing the BE-0337 changes and reproducing the identical 23-test set on the base commit. NOT introduced by this pattern, owed to a focused triage story before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
